<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      PINNED, TEMPORARILY, AND THIS MUST GO BACK TO "*" ONCE 0.36.1 IS PUBLISHED.

      "*" is the deliberate canary: it resolves to the newest published stable release, so a sample
      is the first thing to break when a release is wrong. On 2026-08-24 it did exactly its job -
      0.36.0 shipped DocToolkit.dll alone, and these samples went from building to 36 compile
      errors. See F11 / the hotfix PR.

      But the canary firing also blocks the fix: release.yml builds this solution before it packs,
      so with "*" resolving to the broken 0.36.0 there is no way to publish the version that
      repairs it. Pinning to the last good release is the only way out that does not require
      unlisting 0.36.0 first.

      RESTORE THIS TO "*" the moment 0.36.1 is on nuget.org. A pinned sample is a disarmed canary,
      and this repo has already lost that canary for weeks once by leaving a floor here.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Ank.DocToolkit" Version="0.35.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    This sample DOES carry a fixture, unlike every other one here, and the reason is structural
    rather than laziness: a Word 97-2003 binary cannot be produced by the library. The import is
    read-only - AssessLegacyDocWrite reports native .doc saving as unsupported - so there is no
    "build your own input" path the way samples/Presentations has one.

    It is this sample's OWN copy, not a link into tests/. A sample that reaches into the test
    project made a moved fixture surface as an opaque MSBuild copy error once already.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="assets\quarterly-report.doc" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
